\f0\b Request: 
\f1\b0 I am a mechanical engineer helping a facilities manager pick a replacement circulation pump for a building chilled-water loop. Using a synthetic CSV of head, efficiency and NPSH-required data for three candidate pumps, I will use Visual Studio Code to fit a head\'96flow curve for each pump in a Jupyter notebook and intersect it with the system curve to find its operating point. I will then check that the operating point sits within 70\'96110% of best-efficiency flow with at least 1.5 m of NPSH margin, and compare annual energy cost. In Microsoft Word I will write a one-page recommendation memo with the criteria table and operating-point chart. In PowerPoint I will build a two-slide approval deck so the facilities manager can approve the purchase.\
\

\f0\b Task Description: 
\f1\b0 High-level task summary voice-over:\
1. Visual Studio Code: open the prepared Jupyter notebook, load the pump CSV, and check units and ranges.\
2. Visual Studio Code: fit quadratic head, efficiency and NPSH-required curves for each pump and check the fit error.\
3. Visual Studio Code: define the system curve (12 m static head, 50 L/s at 28 m), intersect it with each pump curve to get the operating points, and hand-check one pump.\
4. Visual Studio Code: calculate percent of best-efficiency flow, NPSH margin, shaft power and annual energy cost; mark each pump pass or fail; save the results table and the operating-point chart.\
5. Microsoft Word: fill the one-page recommendation memo with the criteria table, the chart and the recommended pump, then export it to PDF.\
6. PowerPoint: build a two-slide approval deck with the chart on slide 1 and the cost comparison and recommendation on slide 2, then save it.\
